L VOCABULARY\nEach line is [id, operation, dimensions, relation, material, age, optional edit]. The displayed program is a readable serialization of the actual authoritative tuples, not a decorative paraphrase. Dimensions are width/height/depth. mass uses a center position; stack rests on parent top; cap is a triangular prism resting on parent top; attach uses [parent, side \u00b11]; span uses ordered [left anchor, right anchor], deriving its width and height from their bounds. span dimension x=0 means inferred, not zero-width mesh. stone spans realize an arch, other materials realize braced beams. Presets are data only. Suspended court has no dedicated generator. Scene ground is either an island or a channel; these finite composition priors are explicit semantic input, not an inferred ecology.\nMATERIAL REGIONS\nstone: dark mortar core + stagger-free irregular courses and coping; timber: panels + dark posts and belts; copper: large patinated panels. cap: shared pitched silhouette, overlapping courses, ridge, fascia. span: shared arch spandrel or braced deck, partial parapet losses. Openings use projecting frames and dark fields, not Boolean cutouts. age controls clustered stains, missing surface pieces, edge dislocation, attached foliage and debris. No structural stability simulation. Aged cores remain intact; damage is surface/parapet damage, not arbitrary volumetric fracture.\n\nOWNERSHIP / DETERMINISM\nTuples and seed own world state. Topologically ordered relationships resolve in a single pass. Removing an owner suppresses all transitive dependents; restoring it restores them. Lift offsets the selected owner in world Y and propagates through relationships. A lifted attachment can float: this is an intentional relation probe, not physics. A span uses the lower anchor top rather than tilting, so lifting one anchor need not move the span until it becomes the limiting anchor. Each owner's RNG uses scene seed + stable id, preventing edits from shuffling unrelated owners. Draw batches retain owner id, material region and shape; CPU raycast maps an instance to that owner. ground owns terrain, shoreline and ripples. No hidden random calls, animated world time, or camera-dependent population generation. Observer state never regenerates the world. Host cold-mount still resets lab state by original host contract.\n\nVIEWING BAND\n32-degree vertical perspective; minimum orbit radius 55 world units in landscape, 55/aspect in portrait to preserve horizontal scene footprint. Maximum 2.7 times that radius. Typical world width 25, hero footprint around half the landscape width; structure details roughly subpixel to a few pixels. Zoom cannot enter asset inspection distance. Orbit, pan, material changes, mode switches, reseeding, reset and preset selection preserve observer state; Home is the sole explicit pose reset. Resize enforces the distance band only when necessary. Mouse orbit, Shift/right drag pan, wheel zoom; one-finger orbit, two-finger centroid pan + pinch. Static render-on-demand, no continuous render loop.\n\nMECHANISMS / TRANSFER HYPOTHESES\n- Shared surface subdivision with material-specific proportions creates several visual scales from one mass. Applies to all non-cap/non-span masses across presets; transferable candidate, not yet proof across arbitrary prose.\n- Large silhouette + middle-scale courses + fine world-space pigment modulation avoids relying on noise alone. Shader is procedural arithmetic, no sampled images.\n- Dark core under discrete cladding, coping light edges, and directional shadows strengthen contact/readability. Reusable across masonry/timber/copper; openings remain an illusion at grazing angles.\n- Owner-local aging and colonization cluster consequences on their source instead of scattering unrelated decorations. Cheap transferable surface grammar, not ecology or growth simulation.\n- Caps, arch edges and broken parapets carry silhouette information while shader grain carries only pigment. Instancing per owner/material/shape bounds draw calls; shared geometry/materials survive rebuilds and generated geometries are disposed.\n\nCOMPARISON / LIMITS / ARCHAEOLOGY\nMasses mode preserves primary silhouettes, terrain support and lighting but removes details and substitutes neutral material. Water remains a common stage. This measures the aggregate material/detail contribution, not an isolated ablation of each mechanism. It does not remove the semantic roof/arch primitives themselves. Outline is an observer diagnostic.\nNo visual-model calls were used for construction or validation. Perceptual success still needs human inspection. Tiny near-coplanar window fields and moss are distant-view approximations. Terrain is a bounded cluster of coarse rocks, not continuous height-field geology. Arch remains structurally whole beneath damaged parapets. World-space shader phase changes when a parent moves; geometry RNG remains parent stable. Topological tuples are trusted built-in data, not a general parser/editor. CDN Three.js is required, consistent with sibling labs; failed load, script parse, shader compilation, WebGL loss and runtime exceptions surface through independent bootstrap, watchdog and guarded controls. CDN absence is a reported failure, not an offline fallback.\n\nVALIDATION EVIDENCE \u2014 FIRST PASS\nHeadless Chromium 153 / software WebGL: all three presets started without script or shader errors. Fixed-seed detail -\u003e masses -\u003e detail restored identical world signatures; reset restored each preset. Owner material/age/lift edits, transitive remove/restore and repeat reseeding executed. Preset instance counts 2236 / 1902 / 2207; main render draw counts 40 / 50 / 49. Twelve successive reseeds left geometry allocation count at 7. Build times in this test were roughly 9\u201330ms, not an Android benchmark. 390px touch emulation verified orbit, two-finger pan/pinch and camera preservation through comparison. UI stayed within viewport width. Host navigation retained one active iframe and successfully cold-mounted back. Forced CDN rejection and injected module syntax failure both produced visible lab diagnostics. All original lab sources and host bytes were checked unchanged; every embedded executable script was parsed separately. Raster capture had nonempty scene variation, but no visual-model inspection or beauty rating was performed.\nThe environment's browser could not directly fetch CDN modules; runtime tests routed exact Three.js 0.180.0 bytes downloaded from the specified CDN into the browser. Production HTML retains the CDN import. Network portability and actual Android GPU performance remain user-device checks. No claimed external service fallback.\n\nPROJECT BOUNDARY\nThe host and the five earlier labs remain unchanged. No renderer, material, entity, camera or state framework is shared. No new collaboration method is claimed. Preserve this semantic surface as the experiment learns.\n\u003c/script\u003e\u003c/body\u003e\u003c/html\u003e\n"},{"id":"orbital-construction","title":"3D \u00b7 ORBITAL CONSTRUCTION","kind":"Three.js / Modular Orbital Construction Toy","source":"\u003c!doctype html\u003e\u003chtml lang=\"en\"\u003e\u003chead\u003e\n\u003cmeta charset=\"utf-8\"\u003e\u003cmeta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no,viewport-fit=cover\"\u003e\n\u003ctitle\u003eOrbital Construction \u2014 E2 Construction Vocabulary\u003c/title\u003e\n\u003cstyle\u003ehtml,body{margin:0;width:100%;height:100%;overflow:hidden;background:#010204;touch-action:none}canvas{display:block;touch-action:none}#reset{position:fixed;right:16px;bottom:16px;width:44px;height:44px;border-radius:50%;border:1px solid #ffffff38;background:#10151caa;color:#ddd;font:21px system-ui;z-index:3} \n\n#selection{position:fixed;left:50%;top:max(14px,env(safe-area-inset-top));transform:translateX(-50%);\npadding:7px 11px;border:1px solid #ffffff28;border-radius:8px;background:#0c1118c9;color:#e7e